`third_party/nvme2k-1.2.0.0/` is an unmodified copy of https://github.com/techomancer/nvme2k
at commit 0c41727 (release 1.2.0.0), kept as the reference for backports and the amd64
target. Its licence is in `third_party/nvme2k-1.2.0.0/LICENSE`:

    Copyright (c) 2025, Dominik Behr
    All rights reserved.
    (BSD-3-Clause; full text in the LICENSE file)

The code in `src/` derives from nvme2k 1.0.0.2. That release's licence was read in the
upstream history on 2026-10-19, in a clone of https://github.com/techomancer/nvme2k whose
`main` is at 0c41727 (the commit above): tag `1.0.0.2` is commit
cbbadf041d186d084c55d240b3b4d10fb4cc1f07 (2025-11-13, "release 1.0.0.2 ..."). Its `LICENSE`
(blob cb976bfe2b796b88826592e5a39ccbb3059010d3, unchanged since the first commit, ccf25e0)
is the BSD-3-Clause text, its three conditions and disclaimer word for word those of
1.2.0.0, but its copyright line is the template's unfilled placeholder,
"Copyright (c) <year>, <copyright holder>". Its `README.md` says "This project is licensed
under the **3-Clause BSD License**. See the [LICENSE](LICENSE) file for the full license
text." Its source files carry no licence header. Commit eb31419 (2025-12-02, "actually filled
the info") replaced the placeholder with "Copyright (c) 2025, Dominik Behr" and changed
nothing else in the file. So nvme2k 1.0.0.2 is BSD-3-Clause on the same terms as 1.2.0.0,
and Stor98 names its author with the copyright line he filled in later.

`inf/stor98-x64.inf` and the NT sections of `inf/stor98-x86.inf` derive from nvme2k
1.2.0.0's `WDM/NVMe2K.inf` (in `third_party/nvme2k-1.2.0.0/`), whose header reads
"Copyright (c) 2025 Dominik Behr + Ray Hinchliffe"; `LICENSE` names both. The Windows
9x sections of `inf/stor98-x86.inf` derive from SweetLow's `nvme9x.inf` (below).

## nvme9x / nvment (SweetLow)

`src/` is SweetLow's nvme9x/nvment source (https://github.com/LordOfMice/Tools,
`nvme2k-src.zip`, sha256 272b50836d7f9fe4c1a483f5868d099604916ccd4c89b4daacbc1d17c80bb4f2),
imported unmodified except that the bundled Microsoft DDK headers `SCSI.H` and `SRB.H`
were left out and BSD-3-Clause licence headers were added. Its readme is
`src/README-nvme9x.txt`.

Licence, as stated by SweetLow on 2026-10-14 in reply to the maintainer's request:

- the `nvme2k.mpd` code and binary (SweetLow's port and changes): BSD-3-Clause, the licence
  of the original nvme2k project;
- `NVMEVSD.VXD`: public domain. Its source is not part of `nvme2k-src.zip` and is not in
  this repository.

## Microsoft DDK headers

Not included. The Windows 9x build needs `SCSI.H` and `SRB.H` from the Windows 98 or NT4
DDK; place them in `tools/w98ddk/inc/` (git-ignored). The other kits (MSVC 6.0, Windows 2000
DDK, WDK 7.1) live in `tools/` too; see `docs/BUILD.md`.

## CM interface header

`include/stor98cm.h` is original to this project (Yeo Kheng Meng, BSD-3-Clause). It defines
the interface between xhci98's connection manager and stor98 and is licensed on its own as
an interface definition, so that xhci98 (GPL-2.0) can include it unchanged; the file says
so in its header.
